;; si's divergences (doc/scheme/SCHEME-SURVEY.md, "As built: SM5 (write-ahead)", "As built: SCS3"): the classes every non-pass
;; item of bin/si check falls into, each by design, deferred or not applicable, with why and where that was decided. An item is
;; "KIND PROGRAM DETAIL" (arbace.scheme.check). Since SCS3 each program has one judge: the R6RS suites' programs their own report
;; (fails: a test the report says failed, EXPR => RESULT; count: a report of another count of tests than the program's :tests;
;; status: a report and then a non-zero status; no-report: no report, the error's kind), si's own programs their golden output
;; (crash: another status; differs: stdout differs, its first differing line), Larceny's Chez's recorded run (crash, differs,
;; error-differs). An item's class is the first whose :match finds it. bin/si divergences lists them. Goi's format
;; (test/goi/divergences.edn). Read with the Clojure reader.
[
 {:id :chez-conditions
  :title "Chez's own condition types"
  :kind :design
  :why "Chez adds &format, &continuation (and $&src) parts to the conditions its primitives raise; si raises R6RS's (&assertion, &who, &message, &irritants), as the report specifies"
  :decided "§5.3"
  :match [#"^differs larceny/exceptions line 1: .*&format"]}
 {:id :chez-exact-zero
  :title "Chez's exact-zero rules in division and angle"
  :kind :design
  :why "si's tower is Chez's (§5.4; SM5: \"Chez's exact-zero rules\"): an exact zero divisor raises &assertion whatever the dividend ((/ 1.0 0), (/ 0.0 0)), an exact zero dividend gives exact 0 ((/ 0 3.5), (/ 0 0.0)), and the angle of a positive real is exact 0 ((angle +inf.0)); Racket's suite expects the inexact results, +inf.0, 0.0, +nan.0, +nan.0 and 0.0. Chez's run fails the same five tests (bin/si own: chez-fails-too). The same rule is si/data's last line, (/ 1. 0) raising, in its golden output"
  :decided "§5.4, SM5, SCS3"
  :match [#"^fails racket/base \(/ 1\.0 0\) => #\[err #<compound condition>\]$"
          #"^fails racket/base \(/ 0\.0 0\) => #\[err #<compound condition>\]$"
          #"^fails racket/base \(/ 0 3\.5\) => 0$"
          #"^fails racket/base \(/ 0 0\.0\) => 0$"
          #"^fails racket/base \(angle \+inf\.0\) => 0$"]}
 {:id :chez-console-port
  :title "the console port is an input/output port"
  :kind :design
  :why "As in Chez, current-input-port and current-output-port at start are the console's textual input/output port, so (output-port? (current-input-port)) and (input-port? (current-output-port)) are #t; R6RS's (rnrs io ports) requires a textual input port and a textual output port, not that neither be the other too, and Racket's suite expects #f. Chez's run fails the same two tests (bin/si own: chez-fails-too)"
  :decided "§5.5, SCS3"
  :match [#"^fails racket/io/ports \(output-port\? \(current-input-port\)\) => #t$"
          #"^fails racket/io/ports \(input-port\? \(current-output-port\)\) => #t$"]}
 {:id :chez-condition-message
  :title "a lexical violation's message is a format string"
  :kind :design
  :why "As Chez's reader, si's raises a lexical violation whose &message is a format control string, \"~? at char ~a of ~s\", its arguments the &irritants (§5.3: si's conditions have Chez's parts); R6RS specifies a condition's types, not its message's text, and Racket's suite expects Racket's own words (\"out of range escape: ...\"). Chez's run fails the same test (bin/si own: chez-fails-too)"
  :decided "§5.3, SCS3"
  :match [#"^fails racket/exceptions \(guard \(con \(\(violation\? con\) \(display \(condition-message con\)\) \(quote violation\)\)\) \(read \(open-string-input-port \"\\\\xDDDD;\"\)\)\) => \"~\? at char ~a of ~s\"$"]}
 {:id :suite-ill-formed
  :title "a program of Racket's suite that is ill-formed"
  :kind :na
  :why "Racket's R6RS suite (at its pin) has two run programs that are not programs of the language: run/run.sps imports (tests r6rs run), a library the suite does not have, and run/test.sps calls run-test-tests, which (tests r6rs test) does not export (it exports run-test). R6RS makes both a syntax violation found while the program is expanded, before it runs, and si raises it, as Chez does (bin/si own: chez-errors-too): no test of the program is reached, so the case is none of the eval level's"
  :decided "SCS3"
  :match [#"^no-report racket/run status 255: \[:library-not-found \"\(tests r6rs run\)\"\]$"
          #"^no-report racket/test status 255: \[:unbound-identifier \"run-test-tests\"\]$"]}
]
